+\subsection{Recorded-Observation Deployment Cost}
+\label{app:recorded_cost}
+
+We profile our fixed WARM checkpoint and the released FastWAM checkpoint through their deployment implementations on one H100 80GB, with BF16 and batch size one.
+Both receive the same 100 recorded current observations from five Press Button development episodes (20 replanning prefixes each), with 20 additional warmup queries per implementation.
+We fix action horizon 32, execution prefix 4, and 10 flow evaluations.
+Future observations are discarded before inference; WARM's episode memory receives the recorded executed actions, and generated actions are never executed.
+This measures inference cost on a fixed observation cohort, rather than closed-loop success or a matched-training performance comparison.
+
+\begin{table}[h]
+\centering\small
+\caption{\textbf{Recorded-observation deployment cost.} CUDA-synchronized latency over the full deployed replanning path, after the declared warmup. Memory is the maximum PyTorch allocated/reserved GPU memory over the 100 measured queries; it excludes CPU bank storage and system RAM.}
+\label{tab:recorded_cost}
+\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
+\toprule
+Implementation & Median (ms) & p95 (ms) & Allocated (GiB) & Reserved (GiB) \\
+\midrule
+FastWAM release & 366.3 & 381.8 & 23.31 & 23.58 \\
+WARM & 530.1 & 565.2 & 23.62 & 23.74 \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+The WARM bank contains 89,680 events, with exact CPU cosine retrieval and $K=32$; gathered candidates are transferred to the model device.
+Recent-event and action-summary capacities are 20 and 8, respectively, while their observed occupancies are 0--8 and 0--8.
+The recent-event buffer does not saturate in this cohort, so this is not a worst-case history-length measurement.
+The median latency ratio is 1.447 (WARM/FastWAM).
+
+\paragraph{Timing boundary and initialization.}
+We synchronize CUDA immediately before and after the whole deployed replanning call, including image/state preprocessing, retrieval and memory operations where present, generation, and queue publication.
+Disk decoding and simulator execution are excluded.
+Both implementations use a $[1,3,384,320]$ image composite with their native numerical preprocessing.
+WARM's call includes built-in policy telemetry; external evidence writing for the FastWAM reference is outside its timed call.
+The latency difference therefore compares the deployed implementations, including this instrumentation difference, and is not a module-level attribution.
+One-time policy construction, including strict artifact checks and loading on the AFS-backed deployment, takes 854.9\,s for WARM and 144.3\,s for FastWAM in this run; it is recorded separately from query latency.
+No other GPU compute process is observed in either run at five-second sampling; this observation is not a hardware-exclusivity guarantee.
+
+\paragraph{Scope.}
+The FastWAM row is a release-checkpoint compute reference, not a same-data fine-tuned accuracy baseline.
+This profile does not establish a success--cost trade-off, MemoryWAM's cost, robot control frequency, or the cost with a fully occupied 20-event recent memory.
+Those comparisons require their own matched experiments.
+
